# Steamworks: Keys and Steam credentials

The publisher Web API key and the build account's password never go into the game, its repository or build scripts: keep the key on a server or in an untracked .env, log steamcmd in once by hand, and never commit ssfn or config.vdf files.

## A Web API key is in the game's files (error)

- Why: A publisher Web API key in a game or its repository can be read by anyone who has a copy, and it can change your apps.
- Fix: Remove the key, create a new one in Steamworks (the old one is exposed), and call the Web API from a server.
- Source: https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/webapi_overview/auth

## A build script logs steamcmd in with a password (error)

- Why: The build account's password in a script or CI file can be read by anyone with access to it.
- Fix: Log steamcmd in once by hand (steamcmd +login <account>) and use only the account name in scripts.
- Source: https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/sdk/uploading#automating_steampipe

## Steam login files are in the project (error)

- Why: ssfn and config.vdf files hold a Steam login session; with them someone else can sign in as that account.
- Fix: Delete them from the project (and its history), and keep them out with .gitignore.
- Source: https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/sdk/uploading#automating_steampipe

With the steamworks-mcp server connected, `check_code(path)` finds these problems in the whole project, with file and line (`check_code(path, rules=["steamworks-secrets"])` for this group only).
